WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.620
We have a big epidemic with police officers
and deputies shooting dogs.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.400
I mean I would bet you that 95% of law enforcement
on the street today

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.380
have never received this kind of training
or information, they do not have it.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:29.340
You know, I come from the old school where
I started my police career back in the early 70s

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.520
and back then I was taught to us to neutralize
a dog.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.740
Dogs were just considered property.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.660
And in society today it's changed and dogs
are not just property, they're part of the family

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.780
and unfortunately our profession hasn't caught
up to that yet

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.800
and so that's why we're working really hard
to try to get this training

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.840
so officers now can have it and know what's
going on.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.880
Because when an officer ends up shooting a dog

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.400
it really rips right at the fabric of the community.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.920
All you have to do is google 'police shoots
dog' or 'deputy shoots dog'

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.570
there's a website called Puppycide that's
documenting every shooting

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.780
that's putting every one on page.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.840
This problem needs to be solved.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.560
So we talked to VirTra, which is one of the
manufacturers of the virtual training

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.080
and said 'would you be willing to work with
us on this?'

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.300
'would you be willing to take the time to
add these scenarios

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.700
to your already, you know, already good library
of situations that law enforcement handles.'

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.360
And they were very receptive of it.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.600
-report of a subject down, possible medical
condition.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.170
Get on doggie, get, sir, sir are you okay?

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.420
I could sit in a class and talk to you and
tell you how dogs behave

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.480
and I can show you some videos

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.480
but when you can step in this 360 environment
and you see a dog coming at you

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.140
and depending on how you act is how the scenario
is going to go

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.600
there's nothing that you can do any better
than that.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.020
Have you seen my dog? a black-

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.240
He ran around that way.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.540
He ran off that way.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:05.840
Hey, hey dog stay!

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.340
Can you control your dog then?

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.819
We need to make sure that officers understand
dog behavior

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:16.840
because if you understand the way that dog
behaves, the chances of you shooting are slim to none.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.920
Alright, sir we need your dog to calm down.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.920
What are you doing?

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.700
I said he doesn't bite, he's not dangerous!

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.740
You can't wait until after an incident happens

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.860
and then everybody rushes out to get the training
because the damage is done.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.800
And that's why we took on this challenge

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.860
because we realized that law enforcement didn't
get it.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.599
So if we can teach officers dog behavior

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.160
and things that are gonna help them when they
get in these encounters

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.080
it makes them and everyone else safe

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.060
and it keeps the life of a pet in that family
where it belongs.

